    "details": {
      "alignment": "neutral",
      "type": {
        "value": "humanoid",
        "subtype": "goomba"
      },
      "cr": 0,
      "biography": {
        "value": "<h1>Goomba — The Ones Who Die First</h1><p>The Goomba is the most common, most expendable, and most tragically undervalued member of the Koopa Troop's military structure. They are small mushroom-like creatures with no arms, stubby legs, prominent eyebrows that convey a permanent expression of mild indignation, and a combat life expectancy measured in seconds against competent opponents. Mario has personally defeated more Goombas than any living being has defeated anything, and the Goomba species has responded to this statistical reality with the kind of resigned determination that characterizes organisms whose survival strategy is volume rather than individual resilience.</p><p>Goombas serve as the Troop's conscript infantry—the bodies that fill formations, the patrols that walk corridors, the first wave that absorbs enemy attention while more valuable units position themselves. Their combat contribution is primarily absorptive: they exist to be engaged, to occupy enemy actions, and to die in ways that provide tactical information to the units behind them. \"The Goomba learned that the enemy attacks with jumping\" is a genuine entry in Koopa Troop tactical manuals, and nobody in the Troop's command structure considers this entry problematic.</p><p>The Goombas themselves have complicated feelings about their role. Many serve willingly because the Troop provides community, structure, and the sense of purpose that civilian Goomba life—which consists primarily of walking back and forth on platforms for reasons nobody has ever explained—does not offer. Others serve because conscription offers no alternative. And some serve with the quiet defiance of creatures who know they're cannon fodder and have decided to be the best cannon fodder possible, approaching their inevitable defeats with a professionalism that their commanders don't appreciate and that historians will never record.</p>",
        "public": "A small mushroom creature walking a patrol route with an expression of permanent mild annoyance, as though it knows exactly how expendable it is and has opinions about this that nobody asks."
      },
